<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-trigonometry-and-the-unit-circle" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Trigonometry and the Unit Circle</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurement and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.5 Trigonometry II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Determine trigonometric ratios of angles using a unit circle
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    12 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To discover, through coordinate geometry, the equation of a circle centred at the origin and use it to motivate the unit circle.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Avoid revealing the equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m> too early<mdash/>let Learners conjecture it from the specific case <m>r = 5</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="points-on-a-circle">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In groups of 2-4, work through the following questions on graph paper.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            On a set of axes, plot the point <m>P = (3, 4)</m>. Using a ruler, measure the distance from the origin <m>O = (0, 0)</m> to <m>P</m>. What do you find?
                        </p>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Learners should measure (or calculate via Pythagoras) that <m>OP = 5</m> units. If they reach for a ruler, that is fine<mdash/>but prompt them to verify by computing <m>\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now plot the point <m>Q = (4, 3)</m>. How far is <m>Q</m> from the origin?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>OQ = \sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = \sqrt{25} = 5</m> units. So <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are both exactly 5 units from the origin.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Can you find <em>another</em> point with whole-number coordinates that is exactly 5 units from the origin? How many such points can you find in total?
                        </p>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Encourage Learners to look beyond the first quadrant. They should find eight points with whole-number coordinates: <m>(\pm 3, \pm 4)</m> and <m>(\pm 4, \pm 3)</m>. Some Learners may also spot <m>(\pm 5, 0)</m> and <m>(0, \pm 5)</m>, giving twelve in total.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Plot all of the points you found in part (c) on the same set of axes. If you joined all the points that are exactly 5 units from the origin (not just those with whole-number coordinates), what shape would you get?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                A <em>circle</em> of radius 5 centred at the origin.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Suppose <m>(x, y)</m> is <em>any</em> point on this circle. Write down an equation that <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> must satisfy.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <md><mrow>x^2 + y^2 = 25</mrow></md>
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                This comes directly from Pythagoras' Theorem: the distance from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(x, y)</m> is <m>\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m>, which must equal 5.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                If Learners are stuck, prompt them to draw a right triangle with vertices at <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(x, 0)</m>, and <m>(x, y)</m>, and apply Pythagoras.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now suppose your circle has a different radius<mdash/>say, radius <m>r</m>. What equation must any point <m>(x, y)</m> on this circle satisfy?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <md><mrow>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</mrow></md>
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            In particular, what is the equation of the circle of radius <m>1</m> centred at the origin?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <md><mrow>x^2 + y^2 = 1</mrow></md>
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                This special circle<mdash/>of radius 1 centred at the origin<mdash/>is called the <term>unit circle</term>, and we will spend the rest of this lesson exploring it.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <exploration>
        <p>
            Use the interactive below to drag the point around the unit circle. Pay attention to how the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates of the point change as the angle <m>\theta</m> (measured from the positive <m>x</m>-axis) increases.
        </p>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-unit-circle" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="2:1" source="jsxgraph/unit-circle.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-unit-circle" surface="jsxboard" aspect="2:1"/>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To connect the unit circle equation to the trigonometric ratios <m>\sin</m> and <m>\cos</m>, and to derive the Pythagorean Identity.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Anchor the discussion in the equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> that Learners derived in the activity. Introduce <m>\cos\theta</m> and <m>\sin\theta</m> as labels for the coordinates Learners have already met<mdash/>not as new objects.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A common mistake is to read <m>\cos^2\theta</m> as <m>\cos(\theta^2)</m>. Stress that it means <m>(\cos\theta)^2</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </axiom>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>From the Unit Circle to Sine and Cosine</title>
        <p>
            Consider any point <m>P = (x, y)</m> on the unit circle in the first quadrant, and let <m>\theta</m> be the angle that the radius <m>OP</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Dropping a perpendicular from <m>P</m> to the <m>x</m>-axis forms a right triangle whose hypotenuse is <m>1</m>, whose side adjacent to <m>\theta</m> has length <m>x</m>, and whose opposite side has length <m>y</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Applying SOH-CAH-TOA in this triangle:
            <md>
                <mrow>\cos\theta = \frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{x}{1} = x</mrow>
                <mrow>\sin\theta = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{y}{1} = y</mrow>
            </md>
            So the coordinates of any point on the unit circle at angle <m>\theta</m> are simply <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Substituting <m>x = \cos\theta</m> and <m>y = \sin\theta</m> into the unit-circle equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> gives
            <md><mrow>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = 1.</mrow></md>
            This relationship<mdash/>called the <term>Pythagorean Identity</term><mdash/>holds for <em>every</em> angle <m>\theta</m>, and is one of the most useful identities in trigonometry.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <insight>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>unit circle</term> is the circle of radius <m>1</m> centred at the origin. Its equation is
                        <md><mrow>x^2 + y^2 = 1.</mrow></md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For an acute angle <m>\theta</m> measured from the positive <m>x</m>-axis, the point on the unit circle at angle <m>\theta</m> has coordinates
                        <md><mrow>(x, y) = (\cos\theta, \sin\theta).</mrow></md>
                        That is, the <m>x</m>-coordinate <em>is</em> the cosine of the angle, and the <m>y</m>-coordinate <em>is</em> the sine.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Substituting these into the equation of the unit circle gives the <term>Pythagorean Identity</term>:
                        <md><mrow>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = 1.</mrow></md>
                        Here, <m>\cos^2\theta</m> is shorthand for <m>(\cos\theta)^2</m>, and similarly for <m>\sin^2\theta</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Reading Sine and Cosine off the Unit Circle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The point <m>P = \left(\dfrac{1}{2}, \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m> lies on the unit circle. Let <m>\theta</m> be the (acute) angle that the radius <m>OP</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Find <m>\cos\theta</m> and <m>\sin\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Since <m>P</m> lies on the unit circle, its coordinates are <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m>. Comparing with the given coordinates:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos\theta = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\sin\theta = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                (You may recognise this as the point corresponding to <m>\theta = 60°</m>.)
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Using the Pythagorean Identity</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>\theta</m> is an acute angle with <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{5}{13}</m>. Use the Pythagorean Identity to find <m>\sin\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Substitute <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{5}{13}</m> into the Pythagorean Identity:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta \amp = 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>\left(\frac{5}{13}\right)^2 + \sin^2\theta \amp = 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{25}{169} + \sin^2\theta \amp = 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>\sin^2\theta \amp = 1 - \frac{25}{169} = \frac{144}{169}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\sin\theta \amp = \pm\frac{12}{13}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>\theta</m> is acute, its sine is positive, so
                <md><mrow>\sin\theta = \frac{12}{13}.</mrow></md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Recovering a Special Angle from the Unit Circle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A point <m>P = (x, y)</m> on the unit circle lies on the line <m>y = x</m> in the first quadrant. Find the exact coordinates of <m>P</m>, and use them to write down <m>\cos 45°</m> and <m>\sin 45°</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>P</m> lies on both the unit circle and the line <m>y = x</m>, so it satisfies
                <md>
                    <mrow>x^2 + y^2 \amp = 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>y \amp = x</mrow>
                </md>
                Substituting <m>y = x</m> into the first equation:
                <md>
                    <mrow>x^2 + x^2 \amp = 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>2x^2 \amp = 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>x^2 \amp = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>x \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
                (We take the positive root because <m>P</m> is in the first quadrant.) Hence <m>y = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m> too, so
                <md><mrow>P = \left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right).</mrow></md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Because <m>P</m> lies on the line <m>y = x</m>, the radius <m>OP</m> bisects the first quadrant, so the angle it makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis is <m>45°</m>. Reading off the coordinates:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos 45° = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\sin 45° = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise xml:id="unit-circle-points">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each of the following points, determine whether it lies on the unit circle. Show your working.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\left(\dfrac{3}{5}, \dfrac{4}{5}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\left(\dfrac{1}{2}, \dfrac{1}{2}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, \dfrac{1}{2}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\left(\dfrac{2}{3}, \dfrac{\sqrt{5}}{3}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Yes: <m>\left(\dfrac{3}{5}\right)^2 + \left(\dfrac{4}{5}\right)^2 = \dfrac{9}{25} + \dfrac{16}{25} = 1</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>No: <m>\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2 + \left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2 = \dfrac{1}{2} \neq 1</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Yes: <m>\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)^2 + \left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2 = \dfrac{3}{4} + \dfrac{1}{4} = 1</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Yes: <m>\left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^2 + \left(\dfrac{\sqrt{5}}{3}\right)^2 = \dfrac{4}{9} + \dfrac{5}{9} = 1</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="sin-cos-from-point">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The point <m>P = \left(\dfrac{8}{17}, \dfrac{15}{17}\right)</m> lies on the unit circle in the first quadrant. Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle that <m>OP</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Verify that <m>P</m> is on the unit circle.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Write down <m>\cos\theta</m> and <m>\sin\theta</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Check your answers to (b) by computing <m>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\left(\dfrac{8}{17}\right)^2 + \left(\dfrac{15}{17}\right)^2 = \dfrac{64 + 225}{289} = \dfrac{289}{289} = 1</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{8}{17}</m> and <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{15}{17}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = \dfrac{64}{289} + \dfrac{225}{289} = 1</m>, as expected.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="pythag-identity-application">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the Pythagorean Identity to answer each question. Assume all angles are acute.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>If <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{7}{25}</m>, find <m>\cos\theta</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>If <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{1}{3}</m>, find <m>\sin\theta</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>If <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>, find <m>\sin\theta</m>. What is the value of <m>\theta</m>?</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\cos^2\theta = 1 - \left(\dfrac{7}{25}\right)^2 = 1 - \dfrac{49}{625} = \dfrac{576}{625}</m>, so <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{24}{25}</m> (positive since <m>\theta</m> is acute).
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\sin^2\theta = 1 - \dfrac{1}{9} = \dfrac{8}{9}</m>, so <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{\sqrt{8}}{3} = \dfrac{2\sqrt{2}}{3}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\sin^2\theta = 1 - \dfrac{3}{4} = \dfrac{1}{4}</m>, so <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>. Since <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m> and <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>, we have <m>\theta = 30°</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

